, and are labeled 'neighbors of node 0,' indicating these nodes are directl=
y connected to node 0.  The text 'degree =3D 3' next to node 0 specifies it=
s degree (number of connections). The right side shows the same graph, but =
with a peach-colored area highlighting a path from node 3 to node 4, traver=
sing through node 2.  This highlighted path visually emphasizes a specific =
traversal sequence within the graph." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"467" height=
=3D"190" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" style=3D"color:transparent" src=
=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/graphs/introducti=
on-to-graphs/image-13-00-1-MXPBYGLZ.svg?dpl=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1o=
V5c"></div></figure>
<p><strong>Attributes:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Directed vs. undirected: in a directed graph, edges have a direction ass=
ociated with them.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Weighted vs. unweighted: in a weighted graph, edges have a weight associ=
ated with them, such as distance or cost.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Cyclic vs. acyclic: A cyclic graph contains at least one cycle, which is=
 a path that starts and ends at the same node.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
